From: bobsixsixsix-boop <304558835+bobsixsixsix-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH DRAFT] soc: qcom: ice: runtime PM for a dedicated ICE node so its power domain can switch off

*** DRAFT. Not compiled, not booted, not tested. Generated against v7.3-rc4 drivers/soc/qcom/ice.c (the tb323fu
*** series 0001-0130 and the y patches do not touch this file). Only for a trial kernel (y10+) with the owner present.
*** Rationale, evidence and the verification list: linux/power/ALWAYS-ON.md section 4.

On kaanapali the Inline Crypto Engine has its own DT node with
power-domains = <&gcc GCC_UFS_PHY_GDSC>, the UFS host's domain (kaanapali.dtsi
crypto@1d88000). The binding requires power-domains for sm8450, sm8550,
sm8650, sm8750, kaanapali, sa8775p, sc7180, sc7280 and qcs8300, so every one
of those SoCs has the same pinned domain. The driver never
enables runtime PM on that device, so genpd lists it as "unsupported" and
genpd_power_off() always counts one device that is not runtime suspended:
the UFS host controller's GDSC can never switch off, however long the host
itself stays runtime suspended (pm_genpd_summary on a Lenovo TB323FU:
gcc_ufs_phy_gdsc on / 1d88000.crypto unsupported / 1d84000.ufs active).

Follow the consumer: the engine is active from probe (qcom_ice_create()
leaves the clocks on until the consumer's first qcom_ice_suspend()), then
qcom_ice_resume()/qcom_ice_suspend() take and drop one runtime PM reference
on the ICE device. No callbacks are needed: the device sits in a genpd, whose
runtime callbacks only book the domain on and off. The legacy binding, where
the engine is created on the consumer's own device ("ice" reg range), is left
alone so the UFS host's own reference count is not touched from inside its
suspend path.

Expected on the tablet (screen off, UFS idle, flight recorder stopped):
  pm_genpd_summary: 1d88000.crypto suspended, 1d84000.ufs suspended, gcc_ufs_phy_gdsc off-0
  clk_summary: gcc_ufs_phy_ice_core_clk enable_cnt 0 (as before), gcc_gpll4 enable_cnt 0
Test plan before trusting it: 50 forced suspend/resume cycles of the UFS host under I/O (screen on), fstrim,
a 1 h dd read/write loop, three s2idle cycles, then an unattended night; abort on any ufshcd/ufs_qcom/ice error
in dmesg. Failure mode to expect: UFS not coming back after the first screen-off idle -> root filesystem hangs.
Reviewer note (2026-10-16): ufshcd reprograms inline-crypto keyslots only in ufshcd_hba_start() (ufshcd_crypto_enable),
not on a hibern8-only runtime resume, so keyslot contents across a real GDSC off rely on the GDSC's RETAIN_FF_ENABLE,
exactly as legacy-binding SoCs (ICE inside the UFS node, e.g. sm8250) already do today. Nothing on this install uses
inline crypto (inventory/mounts has no encrypted mount), so that path is NOT exercised by the test plan above; say so
if this is ever sent upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM [Claude Code]
---
 drivers/soc/qcom/ice.c | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/ice.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/ice.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/xarray.h>
 
 #include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h>
@@ -113,6 +114,8 @@
 	bool use_hwkm;
 	bool hwkm_init_complete;
 	u8 hwkm_version;
+	/* dedicated DT node: runtime PM follows qcom_ice_resume()/_suspend() */
+	bool rpm;
 };
 
 static DEFINE_XARRAY(ice_handles);
@@ -315,19 +318,33 @@
 	struct device *dev = ice->dev;
 	int err;
 
+	if (ice->rpm) {
+		err = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
+		if (err < 0) {
+			dev_err(dev, "Failed to runtime resume: %d\n", err);
+			return err;
+		}
+	}
+
 	err = clk_prepare_enable(ice->core_clk);
 	if (err) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to enable core clock: %d\n", err);
-		return err;
+		goto err_put;
 	}
 
 	err = clk_prepare_enable(ice->iface_clk);
 	if (err) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to enable iface clock: %d\n", err);
-		return err;
+		clk_disable_unprepare(ice->core_clk);
+		goto err_put;
 	}
 	qcom_ice_hwkm_init(ice);
 	return qcom_ice_wait_bist_status(ice);
+
+err_put:
+	if (ice->rpm)
+		pm_runtime_put(dev);
+	return err;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_ice_resume);
 
@@ -337,6 +354,10 @@
 	clk_disable_unprepare(ice->core_clk);
 	ice->hwkm_init_complete = false;
 
+	/* lets the power domain this node sits in switch off with its consumer */
+	if (ice->rpm)
+		pm_runtime_put(ice->dev);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_ice_suspend);
@@ -738,6 +759,7 @@
 	unsigned long phandle = pdev->dev.of_node->phandle;
 	struct qcom_ice *engine;
 	void __iomem *base;
+	int ret;
 
 	guard(mutex)(&ice_mutex);
 
@@ -756,6 +778,26 @@
 		return PTR_ERR(engine);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A dedicated ICE node may sit in a power domain (kaanapali: the UFS
+	 * host's GCC_UFS_PHY_GDSC). genpd powers a domain off only once every
+	 * device in it is runtime suspended, so without runtime PM here the
+	 * domain stays on for as long as the ICE is probed, whatever its
+	 * consumer does. Follow the clocks: active from probe (qcom_ice_create()
+	 * left them on) until the consumer's first qcom_ice_suspend(), then
+	 * along qcom_ice_resume()/qcom_ice_suspend(). The legacy binding creates
+	 * the engine on the consumer's own device and is left alone.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_get_noresume(&pdev->dev);
+	pm_runtime_set_active(&pdev->dev);
+	ret = devm_pm_runtime_enable(&pdev->dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		pm_runtime_put_noidle(&pdev->dev);
+		xa_store(&ice_handles, phandle, ERR_PTR(ret), GFP_KERNEL);
+		return ret;
+	}
+	engine->rpm = true;
+
 	xa_store(&ice_handles, phandle, engine, GFP_KERNEL);
 
 	return 0;
